[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Auditing in Oracle Database"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Auditing in Oracle Database allows you to track database activity. It provides a way to monitor and record specific database operations, such as user logins, table access, and data modifications. This information is crucial for security, compliance, and troubleshooting."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Types of Auditing"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Standard Auditing: Audits database operations based on predefined audit options."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fine-Grained Auditing (FGA): Provides more granular control over auditing, allowing you to audit specific data access based on conditions."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Value-Based Auditing: Captures the values of specific columns when data is modified."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Enabling Auditing"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before you can start auditing, you need to ensure that auditing is enabled. This is typically done at the database level."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"SQL> AUDIT_TRAIL=DB,EXTENDED; -- Modify the init.ora or spfile
SQL> ALTER SYSTEM SET AUDIT_TRAIL=DB,EXTENDED SCOPE=SPFILE;
SQL> SHUTDOWN IMMEDIATE;
SQL> STARTUP;"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'AUDIT_TRAIL' parameter controls where audit records are stored. 'DB' stores audit records in the 'SYS.AUD$' table. 'EXTENDED' includes SQL text and bind variable values in the audit records."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Standard Auditing Examples"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"SQL> AUDIT CONNECT;
SQL> AUDIT CREATE TABLE;
SQL> AUDIT SELECT ON HR.EMPLOYEES BY ACCESS;"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The first statement audits all successful and unsuccessful connection attempts. The second statement audits all 'CREATE TABLE' statements. The third statement audits all 'SELECT' statements on the 'HR.EMPLOYEES' table."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Fine-Grained Auditing (FGA) Examples"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"SQL> BEGIN
  DBMS_FGA.ADD_POLICY(
    object_schema   => 'HR',
    object_name     => 'EMPLOYEES',
    policy_name     => 'SALARY_AUDIT',
    audit_condition => 'SALARY > 10000',
    audit_column    => 'SALARY, EMPLOYEE_ID',
    handler_schema  => NULL,
    handler_module  => NULL,
    enable          => TRUE,
    statement_types => 'SELECT, UPDATE');
END;
/ "},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example creates an FGA policy that audits 'SELECT' and 'UPDATE' statements on the 'HR.EMPLOYEES' table when the 'SALARY' is greater than 10000. It audits the 'SALARY' and 'EMPLOYEE_ID' columns."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Viewing Audit Records"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Audit records are stored in the 'SYS.AUD$' table (or other tables depending on the audit trail configuration). You can query this table to view audit information."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"SQL> SELECT USERNAME, ACTION_NAME, OBJ_NAME, SQL_TEXT, TIMESTAMP FROM DBA_AUDIT_TRAIL WHERE ROWNUM <= 10;"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"This query retrieves the username, action name, object name, SQL text, and timestamp from the 'DBA_AUDIT_TRAIL' view for the first 10 records."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Disabling Auditing"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"SQL> NOAUDIT CONNECT;
SQL> NOAUDIT CREATE TABLE;
SQL> NOAUDIT SELECT ON HR.EMPLOYEES;"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"These statements disable the auditing options that were previously enabled."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"SQL> BEGIN
  DBMS_FGA.DROP_POLICY(
    object_schema => 'HR',
    object_name   => 'EMPLOYEES',
    policy_name   => 'SALARY_AUDIT');
END;
/ "},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"This PL/SQL block drops the FGA policy named 'SALARY_AUDIT' on the 'HR.EMPLOYEES' table."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Considerations"},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Auditing can generate a significant amount of data, so plan your audit strategy carefully."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Regularly review and archive audit data to manage storage space."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Secure the audit trail to prevent unauthorized access or modification."}]
